s N. Caring: a relational approach to ethics and moral education. University of California Press; 2013. [ Google Scholar ] 70. Slote M. The ethics of care and empathy. Routledge; 2007. [ Google Scholar ] 71. Kuhse H. Caring is not enough: reflections on a nursing ethics of care. Aust J Adv Nurs. 1993;11:32–32. [ Google Scholar ] 72. Allmark P. Can there be an ethics of care? J Med Ethics. 1995;21(1):19–24. doi: 10.1136/jme.21.1.19. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 73. Mihailov E. Is deontology a moral confabulation? Neuroethics. 2016;9(1):1–13. doi: 10.1007/s12152-015-9244-5. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 74. Mihailov E. The argument from self-defeating beliefs against deontology. Ethical Perspect. 2015;22(4):573–600. [ Google Scholar ] 75. Forlini C. Empirical data is failing to break the ethics stalemate in the cognitive enhancement debate. AJOB Neurosci. 2020;11(4):240–242. doi: 10.1080/21507740.2020.1830883. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 76. Mertz M, Schildmann J. Beyond integrating social sciences: Reflecting on the place of life sciences in empirical bioethics methodologies. Med Health Care Philos. 2018;21(2):207–214. doi: 10.1007/s11019-017-9792-z. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Additional file 1. Interview Guide (23.5KB, docx) Data Availability Statement The datasets generated and analysed during the current study are not publicly available due to confidentiality and privacy reasons: the transcripts of the interviews contain information from which the identity of the participant can be easily retraced, thus we could include in the manuscript only excerpts of the raw material to support the findings and conclusions, along with the outline of the interview questions. However, the datasets are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.',4,'20