5, 2022. van Herten, J., B. Bovenkerk, and M. Verweij. 2018. One Health as a moral dilemma: Towards a socially responsible zoonotic disease control. Zoonoses and Public Health 66(1): 26–34. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] Verweij, M., and B. Bovenkerk. 2016. Ethical promises and pitfalls of One Health. Public Health Ethics 9(1): 1–4. Velasquez, M., C. Andre, T. Shanks, and M. Meyer. 2014. Justice and fairness. Markkula Center for Applied Ethics. Santa Clara University. http://www.scu.edu/ethics/ethics-resources/ethical-decision-making/justice-and-fairness/ . Washington, H., G. Chapron, H. Kopnina, et al. 2018. Foregrounding ecojustice in conservation. Biological Conservation 228: 367–374. Wilkin, H. 2020. A failure to communicate, interview by Jennifer Rainey Marquez. Georgia State University Research Magazine . https://news.gsu.edu/research-magazine/a-failure-to-communicate-covid-19-pandemic-public-health-messaging . Accessed January 4, 2022. Williamson, L. 2014. Patient and citizen participation in health: The need for improved ethical support. American Journal of Bioethics 14(6): 4–16. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] Zinsstag, J., E. Schelling, D. Waltner-Toews, and M. Tanner. 2011. From “One Medicine” to “One Health” and systemic approaches to health and well-being. Preventive Veterinary Medicine 101(3–4): 148–156. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] Zurlo, J., D. Rudacille, and A. Goldberg. 1996. The three Rs: The way forward. Environmental Health Perspectives 104(8): 878–880. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ]',5,'2025-10-20 05:54:05','2025-10-20 05:54:05');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6291,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12151256','Development and pilot testing of an empowerment-based educational app for complementary therapies in diabetes: a Delphi study.','Chang HY, Huang YY.','2025','authentication tweetable unlabelled unlabelled alzahrani unverified alzahrani personalizing alzahrani crichton disapproval firkins refutation yukawa settling yukawa baldoni mogueo alzahrani yukawa forwarding endorsements finalization dissent rir amor tailors forwarding rir endorsements dialects rir authentication logout stepanian journaling tuominen dialects fonts authentication nimmanterdwong gung 202102201b0 gung linkou gung gung nstc my3 gung alzahrani paudyal amor girabent farres roses noguer rincon bagur calafat paq healthcare9060655 baldoni aquino sanches giraud fabbro baldoni siren crichton isenring lohning firkins eisfeld keinki buentzel hochhaus huebner harnett sibbritt dihle nordhaug jerpseth tveiten joranger ruud knutsen crandall ricke delis mogueo oga omenka hatem kuate defo edm2 luong nimmanterdwong boonviriya tangkijvanich boyko vollset duprey hagins lindstedt aali abate abate abbasian kangevari kangevari elhafeez rabu anam sumeru sutrisna joim stepanian mendelsohn mariussen heggdal tuominen leino kilpi poraharju cabutto carrion lehtio moreto stolt sulosaari virtanen yukawa tsutani kiuchi gou fcimb','Highlights • Developed the educational app integrating WHO empowerment strategies for managing complementary therapies in diabetes. • Conducted rigorous Delphi method and pilot testing for validation processes, demonstrating high face and content validity, and receiving strong consensus among external experts on the app''s relevance and usability. • Aligned with human-centered design principles for mHealth development, addressing context-specific user needs, specifying requirements, and evaluating design solutions to meet usability and effectiveness criteria. Keywords: Empowerment, Complementary therapies, Diabetes, Educational application (APP), Delphi method Abstract Background Diabetes management often includes complementary therapies alongside conventional treatment, yet patients may lack the knowledge to integrate both safely. Empowering the use of complementary therapies in diabetes management may enhance patients'' understanding of their benefits and risks, support informed decision-making, and improve overall care of diabetes. Objective To develop and pilot test the Empowerment-based Complementary Therapies for Diabetes Management educational application (app) Methods A two-round modified Delphi method, conducted from October 2023 to April 2024, involved 23 external experts and six patients with diabetes. The app was designed using the WHO''s empowerment framework, emphasizing self-control, cognitive, psychological, and behavioral strategies. Expert consensus was achieved via the content validity index and pilot testing focused on functionality and usability in patients with diabetes. Results This app demonstrated acceptable face validity, with a score of 0.88, and content validity, with item-level and scale-level content validity indices exceeding 80 percent. The pilot testing confirmed that the app''s navigation, secure authentication processes, and reliable data storage systems are not only user-friendly but also feasible. Notably, participants showed a significant 9.02 % increase in their understanding of the benefits and risks of using complementary therapies in diabetes, underscoring the app''s educational effectiveness. Conclusions The app is a capable educational tool for empowering patients with diabetes by providing information on complementary therapies and enhancing their ability to integrate these therapies safely with conventional medicine. Tweetable abstract The Educational App for Complementary Therapies in Diabetes empowers patients to integrate complementary therapies safely with conventional medicine What is already known about the topic. • Complementary therapies are commonly used by patients with diabetes alongside conventional treatments, but many patients lack sufficient knowledge about how to integrate these therapies safely. • There is a lack of empowerment-based educational tools that are effective in enhancing patient engagement, self-management, and decision-making in diabetes management. Alt-text: Unlabelled box What this paper adds. • This paper introduces an empowerment-based educational app explicitly designed to improve diabetic patients'' understanding of complementary therapies and their safe integration with conventional treatments. • The pilot testing of the app demonstrated a measurable increase in patients’ ability to assess the benefits and risks of complementary therapies, enhancing informed decision-making in diabetes management. Alt-text: Unlabelled box 1. Introduction Diabetes mellitus, a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by hyperglycemia, presents an escalating global health burden, with prevalence projected to surge from 529 million cases in 2020 to 1.3 billion by 2050 ( Ong et al., 2023 ). Given the chronic and incurable nature of diabetes, effective management requires a multifaceted approach that includes lifestyle modifications, pharmacotherapy, and an increasing emphasis on integrating complementary therapies into conventional treatment regimens ( Alzahrani et al., 2021 ). Complementary therapies encompass a diverse array of healthcare practices, products, and systems outside the realm of conventional medicine. These include nutritional, psychological, and physical approaches, combination therapies, and other complementary health approaches such as traditional Chinese and Ayurvedic medicine ( National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH), 2021 ). Integrating complementary therapies has become a common approach for many patients to enhance control over their condition and improve overall well-being. In Taiwan, the use of nutritional supplements among individuals with diabetes has risen substantially, from 68.3 % in 2007 to 89.9 % in 2023. Despite this increase, a significant proportion of these patients (69.7 %) often do not disclose their use of complementary therapies to healthcare professionals ( Chang et al., 2023 ). Additionally, patients frequently rely on non-professional sources for information about complementary therapies, which may not always provide accurate or validated safety information ( Sari et al., 2021 ). Given the high rate of nutritional supplement use in diabetes management, low professional involvement in communication about complementary therapies, and the potential risks of unverified therapies—including adulteration, side effects, contamination, adverse effects, and herb-drug interactions ( Alzahrani et al., 2021 ; Zheng et al., 2020 ) —there is a critical need to educate patients on the safe and effective use of these therapies. The rising popularity of complementary therapies in diabetes management underscores the necessity for patients to understand their potential benefits and risks. Enhancing patient-provider communication, personalizing treatment plans, ensuring safe integration of therapies, evaluating efficacy, and promoting health literacy regarding complementary therapies are essential for optimizing diabetes care. To address this need, we conducted a study using a modified Delphi method to reach a consensus among an expert panel on the design of an educational application aimed at empowering patients to manage the use of complementary therapies alongside conventional diabetes care. 1.1. Communication of complementary therapies A meta-analysis found that 67 % of individuals did not disclose their use of complementary therapies to healthcare professionals ( Alzahrani et al., 2021 ). Many patients believe that conventional medicine and complementary therapies do not interact with each other, with a strong preference for natural approaches that won’t hurt ( Crichton et al., 2021 ). The literature provides extensive discussion on the communication gaps between patients and healthcare professionals regarding the limited disclosure of complementary therapies use, including fear of healthcare professional disapproval, lack of support from healthcare professionals, patients perceiving uncomfortable discussion, patients perceiving unimportant for disclosure, lack of time, belief in safe complementary therapies and no interfered with conventional treatment ( Chang et al., 2013 ; Ng et al., 2020 ). In fact, 54.9 % of individuals using complementary therapies were at risk of herb-drug interactions due to the concurrent use of antidiabetic herbs and conventional drugs ( Firkins et al., 2018 ). The critical factors associated with the disclosure of using complementary therapies were inquiry from healthcare professionals, trust relationships with healthcare professionals, less fear of refutation, higher health literacy, knowing how to address the issue, and having concerns about the safety of complementary therapies ( Foley et al., 2019 ; Ng et al., 2020 ; Yukawa et al., 2017 ). Although the inquiry from healthcare professionals is a vital factor associated with the disclosure of using complementary therapies ( Ng et al., 2020 ), without substantive health literacy may affect patients capable of actively settling and seeking advice from healthcare professionals regarding complementary therapies to make prudent and appropriate decisions themselves, and to use complementary therapies safely and effectively ( Yukawa et al., 2017 ). 1.2. Empowerment and complementary therapies Patient empowerment is “a process in which patients understand their role, are given the knowledge and skills by their health-care provider” to gain greater control over their health decisions ( World Health Organization, 2009 , p.190). Four strategies being fundamental to the process of patient empowerment are (1) driving forces of self-control: understanding the individual’s role to take self-control; (2) empowering strategies-cognitive: acquisition of sufficient knowledge to process health information; (3) empowering strategies-psychological: a belief that one’s actions can have an actual impact patient skills; and (4) empowering strategies-behavioral: the presence of an action ( Chang, 2020 ; World Health Organization, 2009 ). Empowerment is a well-established predictor of patient outcomes through psycho-logical, habitual, and behavioral changes as a meta-analysis study ( Baldoni et al., 2017 ; Mogueo et al., 2021 ). There is a lack of interventional research on empowerment-based educational programs aimed at improving health literacy and communication regarding the use of complementary therapies, both in national and international literature. A survey found that patients’ perspective of “I wanted them to understand my health status fully” was the most significant cause of disclosing complementary therapies use to both conventional doctors and complementary therapy practitioners ( Foley et al., 2021 ). Patients demonstrate greater empowerment when they seek to better understand the complementary therapies or conventional medicines they are using as well as how to communicate their use with healthcare professionals. Given that empowerment has been empirically demonstrated to significantly influence patients as a cognitive, motivational factor, driving them towards control, mastery, competence building, and goal setting for their health ( Halvorsen et al., 2020 ), a tailored empowerment educational program designed to enhance patient-healthcare professional communication regarding the use of complementary therapies may promote the provision of optimal patient care. This integration is crucial for ensuring patient safety when combining complementary therapies with conventional medicine. In the light of the literature review, concerns about patients’ use of complementary therapies arose in part from the limited quality of available information, the high prevalence of use, and the risks to patient safety associated with self-directed integration of complementary therapies and conventional medicine ( Alzahrani et al., 2021 ; Foley et al., 2019 ). Health literacy is of great importance to patients as it allows them to effectively use information about complementary therapies and communicate their use with healthcare professionals to make an appropriate decision ( Yukawa et al., 2017 ). Therefore, the demands for skill-building educational programs to improve complementary therapy health literacy and communication among patients with diabetes is heightened by the increasing use of complementary therapies among patients with diabetes, the risks of adverse effects and herb-drug interactions due to non-disclosure, the need for knowledge and skills to access accurate and adequate information. Such programs are essential for enabling patients to make informed self-management decisions and avoid the risks associated with unregulated and untested complementary therapies and products. This study aims to develop the Empowerment-based Complementary Therapies for Diabetes Management educational application (app), leveraging expert insights to create a tool that supports patients in making informed decisions about their diabetes management. The objectives of this study are to (1) identify key aspects and features of the educational app through a structured consensus process; (2) discuss and vote on each item of the instructional design to standardize the educational app; (3) reach a consensus on the finalized design and content of the educational app; and (4) conduct pilot testing of the educational app with the target users via a mobile platform. 2. Methods 2.1. Design This study conducted a two-round modified Delphi method to achieve expert panel consensus on the design of the educational app to support the management of complementary therapies in patients with diabetes ( Fig. 1 ). The Delphi process, conducted from October 2023 to April 2024, involved panelists completing a series of online questionnaires and providing feedback via email. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab The process of the Delphi technique used. 2.2. External expert panel The selection criteria for the Delphi panel in the study on developing the educational app included expertise in diabetes management, empowerment approaches, and/or complementary therapies, along with a willingness to dedicate sufficient time for multiple Delphi rounds and practical communication skills. Delphi panels ideally consist of 8 to 23 participants ( Shang, 2023 ). Accordingly, a purposive sampling method was used to recruit at least 20 expert panelists, accounting for a 25 % dropout rate during the Delphi rounds, resulting in an initial target of 25 experts. To ensure diversity and minimize bias, panelists were recruited from various institutions across Taiwan through invitation email. Even when panelists were from the same institution, they were from different departments and remained unaware of who else was invited to participate. A modified Delphi method utilizing anonymous online surveys was employed to ensure equitable participation in developing a consensus-based educational app. The patient panelists for pilot testing were individually approached by our research assistant from multiple diabetes clinics, ensuring they were unaware of the other participants in the pilot group. This process minimized the risk of interaction among patients and maintained the integrity of the data collected. 2.3. Round one - developing the aspects and features of the educational app Drawing on previous research on key factors in the management of complementary therapies for diabetes by healthcare professionals ( Chang et al., 2021 ; Chang et al., 2023 ), the research team developed various aspects and features for the educational app aligned with empowerment dimensions and indicators. These elements were proposed as potential components of the app ( Table 1 ). An online survey was then distributed to panelists to validate these aspects and features and to gather suggestions for their exclusion or revision. Panelists provided anonymous feedback on the suitability and practicality of each aspect and feature of the app designed to support the management of complementary therapies alongside standard diabetes treatment. A 4-point scale was employed to evaluate the adequacy of each aspect and feature (1 = totally inadequate, 2 = inadequate, 3 = adequate, 4 = totally adequate). Additionally, panelists were encouraged to provide comments and suggestions for improvement in an open-ended format. A consensus threshold was set, requiring at least 70 % of panelists to score an item as 3–4 for inclusion or 1–2 for exclusion ( Kim et al., 2013 ). Items that met this consensus were incorporated into the app for the second round. This method ensured a thorough evaluation and refinement process based on expert input, achieving a consensus-based educational app for managing the use of complementary therapies in diabetes. Table 1. App features corresponding to the theoretical empowerment dimensions. Empowerment dimensions Indicators Description App features Driving forces of self-control Health consciousness Tailoring features for specific patients’ needs (personality traits, value systems), interactive features Features supporting perceived relevance to motivate people toward wellness-driven cognition Empowering strategies-cognitive Health knowledge Safety of education on CT 1 , along with appropriate learning strategies and data monitoring Features supporting cognitive resources to their empowerment Empowering strategies-psychological Health credibility & attention identifying credible sources of CT information, implementing risk management strategies, and conducting data analysis Features encouraging people’s empowerment -seeking strategies Empowering strategies-behavioral Behavioral intention Adopting advocated behaviors, communication, and data export & forwarding Features supporting CT communication between HCPs 2 and patients Open in a new tab 1 Complementary therapies. 2 Healthcare professionals. 2.4. Round two - establishing consensus on the content of the educational app In this round, the following processes were achieved: (1) development of instructional designs and content for the educational app based on panelist endorsements; (2) distribution of an online survey to justify these instructional designs and materials; and (3) finalization of the design on the app. The research team created the instructional content from Round One''s endorsed aspects and features. Ensuring that instructional materials were relevant and tailored to meet the needs of the target audience involved an iterative design process where drafts of the instructional materials were reviewed and refined based on feedback from potential users and experts. An online survey asked panelists to rate and comment anonymously on the appropriateness and feasibility of each component using a predetermined scale. Panelists with significantly differing views provided reasons for their dissent or re-evaluated their opinions. Finally, the readability of patient education materials was checked with patients for face validity, and the app underwent pilot testing to assess its functionality, usability, accessibility, and feasibility. 2.5. Data analysis & rigor The data was analyzed using the IBM® SPSS® Statistics version 28. The Content Validity Index (CVI) was utilized to assess the content validity of the items on the educational app, focusing on both individual item content validity [I-CVI= (agreed item)/ (number of experts)] and overall scale content validity (S-CVI), using scale-level content validity index, average [S-CVI/Ave= (sum of I-CVI scores)/(number of items). To enhance the rigor and quality of data in this Delphi study, Consensus was considered reached when panelists'' opinions met the criteria of the relative interquartile range [RIR = (3rd quartile (Q3) – 1st quartile (Q1)/median) x 100) and the coefficient of variation (COV = (standard deviation/mean) × 100) were less or equal to 20 % ( Amor-Barbosa et al., 2021 ). 3. Results 3.1. External expert panel Characteristics of the 23 panel experts and six patients are presented in Table 2 . The panel experts had an average age of 44.65 years and an average of 21 years of professional experience. Most of the experts were female, middle-aged, held at least a bachelor’s degree, and worked as diabetes nurses or advanced practice registered nurses. Among the panelists, 13 had completed formal coursework on evidence-based complementary therapies, while others had received short training. The experts were recruited from nine institutions spanning northern and southern Taiwan, including healthcare professionals such as two physicians and two pharmacists, to ensure geographical diversity and multidisciplinary perspectives. The patient participants had an average age of 53.00 years and an average diabetes duration of 10.67 years. Most patients were female, married, and employed. Table 2. Demographic Characteristics of the Overall Sample (Expert N = 23 & Patient N = 6). Expert Characteristics M SD Patient Characteristics M SD Age 44.65 9..69 Age 53.00 10.30 Years of working experiences 21.76 7.68 Years of Diabetes 10.67 5.05 N % N % Gender Gender Male 2 8.7 Male 2 33.33 Female 21 91.3 Female 4 66.67 Highest Education Highest Education ≤ Bachelor 10 43.4 ≤ Bachelor 5 80.00 > Bachelor 13 56.5 > Bachelor 1 20.00 Positions Marriage Registered Nurses (≤N3) 9 39.13 Single 2 33.33 Advanced practice nurses 8 34.78 Married 4 66.67 Healthcare professionals 4 17.39 Employment Academics 2 8.7 Employed 4 66.67 Retired 2 33.33 Open in a new tab 3.2. Round one- developing the aspects and features of the ETC-diabetes educational app The research team developed various elements and features for the educational app, aligning them with WHO empowerment dimensions and indicators. This included integrating driving forces of self-control and employing cognitive, psychological, and behavioral empowering strategies ( Table 1 ). • Driving Forces of Self-Control: To harness the driving forces of self-control, the app tailors features to individual patients'' health consciousness, thereby motivating wellness-driven cognition. These components consider patient medical conditions, disease control, blood test results, and comorbidities. • Cognitive Empowering Strategies involve enhancing health knowledge, ensuring the safe use of complementary therapies, employing effective learning strategies, and monitoring data. These features support cognitive resources for empowerment, considering the effectiveness and appropriate use of complementary therapies, assessment of adverse effects, and healthcare professional support. • Psychological Empowering Strategies focus on enhancing health credibility and awareness by helping users identify reliable sources of information on complementary therapies, manage associated risks, and analyze relevant data. Empowerment features include the use of official government websites to verify information credibility, as well as educational videos that explain how to assess product labeling, provide guidelines for selecting appropriate therapies, and offer instructions for safe usage. • Behavioral Empowering Strategies encourage the adoption of advocated behaviors, effective communication, and data export and forwarding. Features supporting communication about complementary therapies between patients and healthcare professionals include educational videos on communication strategies and benefit–risk assessment, as well as quizzes that help users evaluate the potential benefits and risks of using complementary therapies and understand associated risk levels. The Item-Content Validity Index (I-CVI) for these instructional materials indicated an acceptable consensus, with a Scale-Content Validity Index for Average (S-CVI/Ave) of 99.13 % ( Table 3 ). These metrics illustrate strong agreement among experts regarding the relevance and appropriateness of the features included in the app. Table 3. Content validity of the ECT-Diabetes educational application. Empowerment dimensions App Round 1 Features I-CVI 1 Round 2 Content I-CVI Round 2 RIR 3 Round 2 COV 4 Driving forces of self-control Patient medical condition 100 % 91.30 % 0 % 17.2 % Disease and control 100 % 100 % 0 % 11.8 % Blood test results 100 % 100 % 0 % 13.7 % Comorbidities 100 % 100 % 0 % 11.1 % Empowering strategies-cognitive Effectiveness of CT 5 100 % 95.65 % 0 % 14.5 % Consideration of CT Usage 100 % 100 % 25 % 12.7 % Assessment of adverse effects 100 % 100 % 0 % 10.1 % Support by healthcare professionals 100 % 100 % 0 % 10.1 % Empowering strategies-psychological Government Websites resources 95.65 % 95.65 % 0 % 8.5 % Clear labeling of CT components 100 % 100 % 0 % 7.4 % Guidelines for CT selection 100 % 100 % 0 % 10.1 % Instructions for CT usage 100 % 100 % 25 % 12.0 % Empowering strategies-behavioral Guidance on communication 100 % 100 % 0 % 7.4 % Explanations of benefit-risk assessment 91.3 % 100 % 16.7 % 10.1 % Benefit-Risk Assessment Quizzes 100 % 95.65 % 4.2 % 8.4 % Whole app S-CVI 2 /Universal Agreement 86.67 % 73.33 % 6.7 % 6.7 % S-CV/Average 99.13 % 98.55 % Open in a new tab 1 Item-Content Validity Index. 2 Scale-Content Validity Index. 3 relative Interquartile Range. 4 Coefficient of Variation. 5 Complementary therapies. 3.3. Round two- establishing consensus on the content of the educational app Based on endorsements from the previous Delphi round, the research team developed instructional designs and content for the app. Each feature incorporates specific learning goals aimed at empowering the target audience to conduct benefit–risk assessments of using complementary therapies and to enhance their ability to communicate this use effectively with conventional healthcare professionals Applying principles of user-centered design, the team prioritized user needs and preferences throughout the development process, considering usability, accessibility, and user experience to enhance engagement and effectiveness. An iterative design process was employed, where drafts of the instructional materials were reviewed and refined based on feedback from patients with diabetes. For example, to address language barriers in the videos, we added local dialects and languages. Additionally, we increased the font size of the educational material to make it more readable for elderly users. We reduced the overall size of the content to make it easier to navigate. This approach ensured the instructional materials were relevant, understandable, and tailored to the specific needs of the target audience. The Item-Content Validity Index (I-CVI) for the instructional materials of the educational app indicated an acceptable consensus among panelists. The Scale-Content Validity Index for Average (S-CVI/Ave) was 98.55 % ( Table 3 ). In Round Two of the Delphi study, the rigor and quality of data were ensured by achieving consensus through specific statistical criteria. Consensus was reached when either the relative interquartile range (RIR) or the coefficient of variation (COV) was <20 %, demonstrating a high level of agreement among panelists. After addressing these concerns and refining the instructional materials, the overall content validity improved significantly. The revised materials achieved a content validity rating of 100 %, indicating that the app is highly relevant, appropriate, and effective for its intended audience. 3.4. Pilot testing of the educational app Pilot testing involved evaluating the app''s functionality, user interface, and accessibility to ensure it is practical, user-friendly, and meets its educational objectives. A total of six diabetic patients participated in the pilot testing, and the face validity score of 0.88 indicates that all features, including quizzes, lessons, and interactive activities, function as intended. The research team assessed the app''s navigation to confirm that users can easily find their way through various sections and features, ensuring a user-friendly interface. Additionally, the user authentication processes, including sign-up, login, and logout, were tested to verify their smooth and secure operation. The team also ensured that data storage and retrieval processes reliably and accurately store and access user progress, scores, and other data, contributing to the app''s overall functionality, usability, accessibility, and feasibility. Finally, participants increased their score by 9.02 % in understanding the benefit-risk of using complementary therapies in diabetes. 4. Discussion This preliminary study employed empowerment theory to develop the educational application (app) focused on complementary therapies for diabetes management. To our knowledge, no previous studies have developed an educational application of this nature. The development and pilot testing of this app provided valuable insights into creating a user-friendly and effective tool for empowering patients with diabetes through education on complementary therapies. Our results indicate that the app has high face validity and content validity, suggesting that the features and instructional materials are relevant and appropriate for the target audience. The strong consensus among external experts, as reflected in the content validity indices (I-CVI and S-CVI), underscores the app''s alignment with empowerment dimensions and indicators. As Shang (2023) reviewed, the use of the Delphi method in health sciences research typically adopts a consensus level between 70 % and 80 %, which is widely considered rigorous. The WHO conceptual framework of empowerment was utilized as the theoretical foundation for building this educational app in this study. A review of empowerment interventions for individuals with chronic diseases revealed that no studies comprehensively addressed all four components, including self-control, cognitive, psychological, and behavioral empowerment strategies. Specifically, components such as enabling patients to engage effectively with healthcare professionals and creating a supportive environment were infrequently reported ( Stepanian et al., 2023 ). The app addresses the WHO’s four components of empowerment ( World Health Organization, 2009 ) and the critical concerns related to the benefit–risk assessment of using complementary therapies ( Chang et al., 2021 ; Chang et al., 2023 ). It supports the overall goal of empowering patients to manage their use of complementary therapies alongside conventional medicine, focusing on patient capacities such as perceived control, self-efficacy, and health literacy, as well as behaviors including participation and self-management. A review study indicated that interactive digital technologies, particularly mobile applications, improve accessibility and provide tailored interventions. These technologies support various aspects of care, such as health monitoring, information provision, self-management, and shared decision-making, through activities like personalized information delivery, journaling, videos, and educational quizzes and games ( Tuominen et al., 2024 ). In this study, the self-control empowerment strategy aims to enhance users’ health consciousness by providing dashboards where users can input and monitor their medical conditions, blood test results, and use of complementary therapies, thereby enhancing their self-control over health and the integration of these therapies with conventional medicine. The cognitive empowerment strategy delivers tailored information to increase health knowledge through comprehensive learning modules covering the benefits and risks of using complementary therapies. The psychological empowerment strategy focuses on building health credibility by helping users locate trustworthy governmental resources and engage with the information provided. The behavioral empowerment strategy fosters behavioral intention by encouraging users to complete quizzes that assess their use of complementary therapies and to adopt and maintain healthy practices. The pilot testing phase, incorporating feedback from patients with diabetes, ensured the app''s functionality and user-friendliness, tailoring it to meet users'' specific needs and preferences. Enhancements like local dialects, larger fonts, and streamlined navigation improved accessibility, especially for elderly users or those with limited health literacy. The user-centered design confirmed that secure authentication processes and reliable data storage are critical for user trust and engagement. Additionally, the app''s intuitive navigation and effective integration of quizzes, lessons, and interactive activities highlight its potential as a valuable educational tool. According to a review study by Nimmanterdwong et al. (2022) , the app aligns well with the four steps of human-centered design in mHealth development for older adults: understanding and specifying the context of use, identifying user requirements, producing design solutions, and evaluating designs against requirements. 4.1. Strengths and limitations The strengths of this study include achieving expert consensus through an external panel and conducting rigorous validation to ensure content credibility. The iterative design process, guided by user feedback, enhanced usability and accessibility. Additionally, comprehensive testing addressed functionality, navigation, and data security, resulting in a robust and reliable app. Limitations in this study included the pilot testing involving a relatively small group of users, which may not fully capture the diversity of the target population, and short-term user engagement, which might not entirely reflect the app''s sustained impact on user empowerment. Future research should involve long-term studies to assess the sustained effect of the educational app on patient empowerment, health outcomes, and the benefit-risk management of using complementary therapies in diabetes. Additionally, expanding the testing to include a more diverse population in terms of age, gender, and cultural background will help refine the app further and ensure its broader applicability. 4.2. Conclusion The Empowerment-based Complementary Therapies for Diabetes Management educational app represents a significant step forward in leveraging technology to empower patients with diabetes through education. This collaborative approach, including panel experts and users, ensured that the instructional materials were relevant and tailored to meet the needs of the target audience. The app''s high face and content validity, combined with its user-centered design and comprehensive testing, indicate its potential as a valuable tool in patient education. These empowerment strategies serve as driving forces for self-control in the use of complementary therapies, enhance users'' knowledge and understanding through cognitive strategies, build confidence in health management through psychological strategies, and promote the practical assessment of benefits and risks to support informed decision-making and effective communication with healthcare professionals. 4.3. Practice implications This educational app is a useful tool for clinical practice, particularly in the management of diabetes through the integration of complementary therapies with conventional medicine. By utilizing empowerment strategies, the app addresses critical aspects of patient education and self-management so that healthcare professionals can incorporate the educational app into their patient education, using it as a tool to facilitate discussions about the benefits and risks of complementary therapies in conjunction with conventional treatment. The use of the app could lead to improved patient-provider communication, as it empowers patients to engage more effectively with their healthcare professionals, ask informed questions, and participate actively in their treatment plans. This empowerment can potentially lead to better health outcomes, increased adherence to treatment regimens, and greater satisfaction with care. Ethical information The study protocol was approved by Chang Gung Medical Foundation Institutional Review Board (No. 202102201B0). CRediT authorship contribution statement Hsiao-Yun Chang: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Visualization, Validation, Supervision, Software, Project administration, Methodology, Investigation, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. Yu-Yao Huang: Writing – review & editing, Resources, Investigation, Funding acquisition, Data curation. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare the following financial interests/personal relationships which may be considered as potential competing interests: Hsiao-Yun CHANG reports financial support was provided by Chang Gung University of Science and Technology. Yu-Yao HUANG reports administrative support was provided by Linkou Chang Gung Memorial Hospital. Hsiao-Yun CHANG reports a relationship with Chang Gung University of Science and Technology that includes: employment. None If there are other authors, they declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Acknowledgments Funding Statement This study was supported by the National Science and Technology Council, Taiwan (NSTC 111-2314-B-255-002-MY3). Acknowledgments The authors acknowledge the panel of experts and patients who contributed to various stages of this research, which was supported by the National Science and Technology Council and Chang Gung University of Science and Technology. Data availability The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author, H.Y.C., upon reasonable request. References Alzahrani A.S., Price M.J., Greenfield S.M., Paudyal V. Global prevalence and types of complementary and alternative medicines use amongst adults with diabetes: systematic review and meta-analysis. Eur. J. Clin. Pharmacol. 2021;77:1259–1274. doi: 10.1007/s00228-021-03097-x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Amor-Barbosa M., Girabent-Farres M., Roses-Noguer F., Ortega-Martinez A., Medina-Rincon A., Bagur-Calafat C. Development and content validity of the physical activity questionnaire-young children (PAQ-YC) to assess physical activity in children between 5 and 7 years. Healthcare. 2021;9:655. doi: 10.3390/healthcare9060655. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Baldoni N.R., Aquino J.A., Sanches-Giraud C., Di Lorenzo Oliveira C., de Figueiredo R.C., Cardoso C.S., Santos T.R., Alves G.C., Dal Fabbro A.L., Baldoni A.O. Collective empowerment strategies for patients with Diabetes Mellitus: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Prim. Care Diabetes. 2017;11:201–211. doi: 10.1016/j.pcd.2016.09.006. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Chang C. Self-control-centered empowerment model: health consciousness and health knowledge as drivers of empowerment-seeking through health communication. Health Commun. 2020;35:1497–1508. doi: 10.1080/10410236.2019.1652385. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Chang H.Y., Chang H.L., Siren B. Exploring the decision to disclose the use of natural products among outpatients: a mixed-method study. BMC Complement. Altern. Med. 2013;13:319. doi: 10.1186/1472-6882-13-319. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Chang H.Y., Huang Y.Y., Chung C.J., Liu F.H. A comparative analysis of complementary therapy use among patients attending diabetic clinics in Taiwan: 2007 vs. 2023. BMC Complement. Med. Ther. 2023;23(1):455. doi: 10.1186/s12906-023-04299-9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Chang H.Y., Lo C.L., Chang H.L. Development of the 